-- The realistic case: the statistics are wrong. Tell the planner q = 1 holds
-- for 5% of the rows, fairly clustered (correlation 0.8: a constant column
-- reports 1, which would make q look like the cheapest Phase A of all), and
-- it puts q right after Phase A, ahead of r and of an OR clause spanning r
-- and a; the data says otherwise and the executor moves q back after the
-- first segfile. In the OR query q ends up behind a as
-- well, since a rejects every row that reaches it; the two disjunct stages r
-- and a themselves are never swapped with each other. The OR clause is
-- re-split against the new order, and the answer is unchanged.
SELECT gio_plant('gp_adapt', 'q', '{0.95,0.05}', 0.8);
 gio_plant 
-----------
 t
(1 row)

